1.18.m:
Bathing and gift-giving are a hundredfold [more fruitful] on the lunar day in Kārttika, O king;
it grants the happiness of attaining Bali’s kingdom and destroys dusty inauspiciousness.
Description of the Puṣpadvitīyā in the Narrative of Śaryāti

1.22.m:
Stopping him, the lord of the gods asked, “Why should you show such rage?”
And he recounted to his father: “The treatise on the marks of men was composed by me;
and for me who made it, an obstacle was caused there, and the marks of women were not [composed].”

1.29.m:
('Om, salutations to Lambarāja.')
Facing the sun, he should mutter [it] eight thousand times;
the Sun becomes a granter of boons.

Whatever he performs with the root mantra succeeds;
all planets become highly pleased.
Going to the city gate, he should inspect the gate [repeating the mantra] eight thousand times.
